      "id": "summary",
      "proposition": {
        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>A mouse detective; a fresh take on the Odyssey; a dangerous wish; and the world’s most watched reality TV show</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://guardianbookshop.com/my-dad-can-9781836008835/?utm_source=editoriallink&amp;utm_medium=merch&amp;utm_campaign=article\">My Dad Can</a> by Stephen Lightbown, illustrated by Claire Sahara Lemp, Quarto, £7.99</strong><strong><br></strong>Iris’s dad can turn into dinosaurs, unicorns, anything she imagines – though some people see Dad’s wheelchair and believe he can’t do anything. This soft-smudged, colourful picture book celebrates the playfulness and creativity of parenthood.</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://guardianbookshop.com/the-fluffy-futon-9798348024208/?utm_source=editoriallink&amp;utm_medium=merch&amp;utm_campaign=article\">The Fluffy Futon</a> by Yuichi Kasano, translated by Cathy Hirano, Gecko, £12.99</strong><strong><br></strong>When Grandma spreads a futon on the sunny porch to air, it’s so fluffy that kittycat, Grandma, hen, chicks and the whole household join each other for a nap in this delightful picture book, perfect for enjoying at bedtime.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jun/26/children-and-teens-roundup-the-best-new-picture-books-and-novels\">Continue reading...</a>"
